## Preserve exactly

- Layout and visual hierarchy (hero, KPI/summary area, month/year navigation, weekday row, date cells, event chips, upcoming bookings side panel, legend, day-detail modal, create/edit/view booking flow from a date)
- Crystal/glass surfaces, borders, shadows, glows, cinematic lighting, and animations
- Sharp event marker/icon styling as last explicitly approved: large crisp SVG hearts for wedding/booked (and anniversary client markers), diamond-ring for engagement, cake for birthday, calendar glyph for other; no invented diamond/premium backend event type. Crystal-glass icon chips with type-colored outer glow only; icons stay vector-sharp (no blur/low-opacity). Keep colors, stroke, size, chips, legend, and this mapping unless the user explicitly requests a new Calendar change.
- Event chips, legend, typography, font sizes, spacing, and responsive layout
- Existing Calendar functionality: `getCalendar` / upcoming list / booking detail APIs as used by this page, date calculations, event-type mappings, occupancy rules (including hiding cancelled occupancy), client birthday/anniversary markers, create-booking-from-date, edit/view from the calendar, permissions, and routes
- Calendar-related tests and their assertions (`aria-label` markers, month labels, occupancy, etc.)

## Other work must stay independent

Work on Login, Dashboard, Clients, Bookings, and other modules without touching the files above.

If you must change shared CSS, layout shells, or shared booking/client files that Calendar *calls* (`bookings-service`, `BookingFormModal`, `BookingViewModal`, `clients-service`, `booking-form` formatters, `api-error`, `cn`):

- Do not alter `.dhara-calendar`, `.dhara-cal-*`, or `calendar-page.css`.
- Scope new styles so they cannot override Calendar descendants.
- Prefer new class names over changing globals used by Calendar.
- Other modules may *call* those shared files, but must preserve Calendar’s current appearance and functionality exactly (including `getCalendar` contracts, occupancy fields, and create-from-date behavior). Do not edit the locked Calendar files listed above without explicit Calendar approval.

## Allowed exception

Only modify the Calendar module when the user clearly asks for a specific Calendar change in that conversation.

